{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792937395,"nonce":"zVmLGpzjVWjgXNgYJZf0Vvvc6dc=","title":"Tiered: LLVM-ladder baseline was scalar and promotions raced warm-up; shared counter vectors and an entry-only quiet gate","message":"With LLVM as tier 2 the shared optimised body kept scalar reductions, so the Cranelift baseline ran reduction loops scalar until LLVM landed: free_function_call (Mac, LLVM held off for the run) 97 ms against 14 ms Cranelift-only. Cold Lua runs on the NUC (taskset 0-3, likely two physical cores with HT, as GitHub's 4-vCPU runners are) were faster Cranelift-only than with the LLVM ladder on almost every row; zylua won 6 of 17 rows against LuaJIT on elapsed with LLVM, 8 Cranelift-only.\n\nChange: the shared body takes counter vectors under an LLVM ladder too (ZYNTAX_LLVM_SCALAR_REDUCTIONS=1 restores scalar), and a promotion counted at a function's entry waits until the program has made no demand on the baseline (stub first calls, interpreted-call requests, interpreted-frame resume points; not speculative callee compiles) for 20 ms, at most 250 ms (ZYNTAX_OPTIMIZE_QUIET_MS). A frame in a hot loop is promoted at once and moves up through OSR.\n\nNUC, 9 interleaved reps, --fast mean against the same build with the window at 0 and scalar reductions (load 2 to 5):\n- zypy: deltablue 0.846, meteor 0.878, spectral-norm 0.910, nbody 0.982, float 0.985, LU 0.998, chaos 1.011, ai 1.028, SOR 1.058, go 1.077, raytrace 1.105. Against PyPy spectral-norm went 7.7/6.8 to 7.0/6.8.\n- zylua (5 reps, elapsed): lambda_call 10.7 -\u003e 4.2 ms, closure_call 10.3 -\u003e 7.3, fib 12.8 -\u003e 12.0; nbody 115.9 -\u003e 128.7, op_overload 65.3 -\u003e 77.9, inlined_call 14.7 -\u003e 19.0; wins against LuaJIT 6 -\u003e 7 elapsed, 5 -\u003e 5 wall.\n\nThe regressions are kernels whose hot non-loop functions want LLVM code early (raytrace's vector methods, go, ai) or whose entry promotion waited behind warm-up (SOR). A fixed window is the crude form: a promotion pays when the function's expected remaining time exceeds the LLVM compile's cost, which per-function counts and compile-size estimates could decide.","files":null}]}